WEBVTT

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:03.690
Analysis is the step that turns collected material into a judgment.

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:07.320
It is where the analyst asks what is known, how it is known, how current it is,

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.240
how relevant it is to the local environment,

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.105
and what action the consumer should consider.

00:00:11.105 --> 00:00:13.200
Analysis is not just summarizing a report.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:14.905
A summary repeats information.

00:00:14.905 --> 00:00:18.075
Analysis evaluates information, connects it to a requirement,

00:00:18.075 --> 00:00:20.995
and states a defensible conclusion with appropriate confidence.

00:00:20.995 --> 00:00:22.605
Source evaluation is central.

00:00:22.605 --> 00:00:25.975
Analysts should ask whether the source has a history of reliable reporting,

00:00:25.975 --> 00:00:28.705
whether the claim is firsthand or repeated from someone else,

00:00:28.705 --> 00:00:32.175
whether the evidence is visible, and whether independent sources corroborate it.

00:00:32.175 --> 00:00:35.850
Corroboration does not mean counting how many posts repeat the same original claim.

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:39.390
It means finding separate evidence that supports or challenges the assessment.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:40.420
Recency matters too.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:44.905
A domain, hash, vulnerability claim, or campaign description may become stale quickly.

00:00:44.905 --> 00:00:47.400
Local relevance matters as much as external reporting.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:50.830
A high-profile threat may not matter equally to every organization.

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:54.905
The analyst should understand affected technologies, exposed services, business units,

00:00:54.905 --> 00:00:58.165
geography, sector, controls, detections, and incident history.

00:00:58.165 --> 00:01:01.910
A threat report becomes more useful when it explains what it means for the environment at

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:02.405
hand.

00:01:02.405 --> 00:01:05.335
Without local context, CTI can become generic noise.

00:01:05.335 --> 00:01:08.600
Confidence should be visible to the consumer, not hidden in the analyst's head.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:12.400
A brief can say that confidence is high because the claim is supported by internal

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.435
telemetry and multiple reliable sources.

00:01:14.435 --> 00:01:18.000
It can say confidence is moderate because the reporting is credible but not locally

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:18.755
observed.

00:01:18.755 --> 00:01:22.495
It can say confidence is low when evidence is limited, old, or indirect.

00:01:22.495 --> 00:01:25.600
This helps consumers choose actions that fit the strength of the evidence.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:30.005
Common mistakes include over-attribution, copying vendor claims without context,

00:01:30.005 --> 00:01:33.785
treating stale indicators as current, confusing possibility with likelihood,

00:01:33.785 --> 00:01:35.105
and ignoring assumptions.

00:01:35.105 --> 00:01:36.770
Attribution is especially risky.

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:40.480
Naming a threat actor may be less useful than explaining observed behavior,

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:43.405
likely objective, affected systems, and defensive action.

00:01:43.405 --> 00:01:45.250
When the evidence is weak, say so.

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:48.145
Inflated certainty can lead to poor decisions, wasted effort,

00:01:48.145 --> 00:01:50.300
and loss of trust in the CTI function.

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:52.585
Careful language helps preserve decision quality.

00:01:52.585 --> 00:01:56.110
Use words such as observed, reported, assessed, likely, possible,

00:01:56.110 --> 00:01:57.375
and confirmed deliberately.

00:01:57.375 --> 00:01:59.305
"Observed" means someone saw evidence.

00:01:59.305 --> 00:02:00.980
"Reported" means a source claimed it.

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:03.195
"Assessed" means the analyst is making a judgment.

00:02:03.195 --> 00:02:05.495
"Likely" expresses probability, not proof.

00:02:05.495 --> 00:02:08.600
"Confirmed" should be reserved for high-confidence facts.

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:10.155
Good intelligence is not loud.

00:02:10.155 --> 00:02:13.665
It is clear about what is known, what is uncertain, and what should happen next.

00:02:13.665 --> 00:02:16.615
A useful review habit is to underline every claim and ask,

00:02:16.615 --> 00:02:19.920
"What supports this?" If the answer is a single weak source, say that.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:23.875
If the answer is internal evidence plus reliable external reporting, say that too.

00:02:23.875 --> 00:02:28.160
Transparent reasoning makes CTI easier to trust and easier to correct when new evidence

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:28.590
appears.
